[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Routing and URL Building in Flask"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Routing is the process of mapping incoming HTTP requests to specific functions in your Python application. In Flask, this is achieved using the app.route() decorator. URL building, on the other hand, allows you to dynamically generate URLs for your functions without hardcoding them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Routing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To register a view function for a URL path, use the route decorator provided by your Flask application instance. The following example demonstrates basic route handling for the root URL."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"from flask import Flask
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return 'Welcome to the Home Page!'"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Variable Rules"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can add variable sections to a URL by marking sections with <variable_name>. Your view function will then receive these variables as keyword arguments. You can also specify converters such as int, float, and path."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@app.route('/user/<username>')
def show_user_profile(username):
    return f'User: {username}'

@app.route('/post/<int:post_id>')
def show_post(post_id):
    return f'Post ID: {post_id}'"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"URL Building with url_for"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Flask provides the url_for() function to build URLs for a specific function. This is vastly superior to hardcoding URLs in your templates or code because it handles application root paths and changes automatically."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"from flask import url_for

@app.route('/')
def index():
    # Generates '/user/john_doe'
    profile_url = url_for('show_user_profile', username='john_doe')
    return f'<a href="{profile_url}">Profile</a>'"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the @app.route() decorator to map URLs to Python functions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Capture dynamic segments using angle brackets like <int:id>."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use url_for() to prevent broken links when restructuring application routes."}]
